  translation: "1. The blessed Lord said: He who performs the action that is his to do, not depending on its fruit, is a renouncer and a yogin — and not only the man who keeps no ritual fire, nor the man who does no rites.\n2. What they call renunciation, know that to be yoga, O Pandava. For no one becomes a yogin who has not given up the aim at the fruit.\n3. For the sage who wishes to climb to yoga, action is said to be the means. For that same man once he has climbed, quiet is said to be the means.\n4. When he does not cling to the objects of the senses, nor to actions, then the man who renounces every resolve is said to have climbed to yoga.\n5. One should lift oneself up by oneself, and not drag oneself down. For the self alone is the friend of the self, and the self alone is the enemy of the self.\n6. The self is the friend of that self by whom the self has been mastered by the self. But for one who has not mastered himself, the self itself acts in enmity, like an enemy.\n7. For the man who has mastered himself and whose mind is at peace, the supreme Self is present as his own self. He is the same in cold and heat, in pleasure and pain, and in honor and disgrace.\n8. He whose mind is content through knowledge and through direct realization, unshaken, with the senses mastered, is called a yogin, one to whom a clod, a stone, and gold are the same.\n9. Toward the well-wisher, the friend, the foe, the indifferent, the neutral, the disliked, and the kinsman, and toward the good and the wicked alike, the even-minded man stands above the rest.\n10. The yogin should keep the mind steadily fixed, staying in a lonely place, alone, with mind and body held in, free of longing, and owning nothing.\n11. Setting his own seat in a clean place, firm, not too high and not too low, with cloth, deerskin, and kuśa grass, the grass underneath.\n12. There, having made the mind one-pointed, with the acts of mind and senses held in, sitting on that seat, he should practice yoga for the cleansing of the mind.\n13. Holding body, head, and neck even and still, steady, letting the gaze rest as it were on the tip of his own nose, and not looking about at the directions.\n14. With the mind deeply at peace, fear gone, established in the brahmacārin's vow, having drawn the mind in, he should sit collected, his thought on me, holding me as the supreme.\n15. Practicing in this way, always, with the mind held in, the yogin reaches the peace whose highest end is release, the peace that rests on me.\n16. Yoga is not for one who eats too much, nor for one who does not eat at all, nor for one given to too much sleep, nor for one who stays awake too long, O Arjuna.\n17. For one whose food and walking are kept in measure, whose effort in actions is kept in measure, and whose sleep and waking are kept to their times, yoga becomes the destroyer of suffering.\n18. When the mind, held to one point, comes to rest in the self alone, and he is free of craving from every object of desire, then he is called concentrated.\n19. As a lamp set out of the wind does not waver, that is the likeness taught of the yogin whose mind is held in, while he practices yoga, concentration on the self.\n20. When the mind comes to rest, checked on every side by the practice of yoga, and when, seeing the self by the self, he rejoices in the self,\n21. that endless joy, which the intellect grasps and which lies beyond the senses, he knows at that time; and once established, this man does not slip from the truth at all.\n22. and having gained which, he thinks no other gain greater than it; established in which, he is not shaken even by a heavy pain.\n23. Know that to be the severing of union with pain, and what is called yoga. That yoga is to be practiced with resolve, by a mind that does not weary.\n24. Having left every desire born of imagination, with nothing remaining, and having drawn the host of the senses in from every side by the mind alone,\n25. Little by little one should draw back, by the intellect held in firmness; and having set the mind firm in the self, one should think of nothing at all.\n26. Wherever the restless, unsteady mind runs out, from that one should check it, and bring this mind under control in the self alone.\n27. For the highest joy comes to this yogin, whose mind has grown deeply quiet, whose passion has died down, who has become Brahman, and who is free of stain.\n28. The yogin whose stain is gone, practicing in this way and ever on the self, easily reaches the endless joy of contact with Brahman.\n29. He sees his own self standing in every being, and all beings in the self. His mind joined in yoga, he sees the same everywhere.\n30. Who sees me everywhere, and sees everything in me: for him I do not vanish, and he does not vanish for me.\n31. Who worships me as abiding in every being, having taken his stand in oneness: that yogin abides in me, even while living in every way.\n32. Arjuna, who by the measure of himself sees pleasure and pain as the same everywhere: that yogin is held to be the highest.\n33. Arjuna said: O Madhusūdana, this yoga which you have taught as sameness — I do not see a steady state of it, because of fickleness.\n34. For the mind is fickle, O Krishna, agitating, strong, and firm. I think restraining it is harder even than restraining the wind.\n35. The blessed Lord said: No doubt, O mighty-armed, the mind is hard to restrain and restless. But it is held, O son of Kuntī, by practice and by dispassion.\n36. Yoga is hard to reach for one whose mind is not under control: that is my view. But for one whose mind is under control and who still strives, it can be reached by the means.\n37. Arjuna said: One who does not strive, yet is endowed with faith, whose mind has slipped from yoga and who has not reached yoga's perfection — what course does he go to, O Krishna?\n38. Fallen from both, does he not perish like a torn cloud, with no footing, bewildered on the path of Brahman, O mighty-armed?\n39. This doubt of mine you should cut away completely, O Krishna. No one other than you can be the one to cut this doubt.\n40. The blessed Lord said: There is no ruin for him, O Pārtha, not in this world and not in the next. For no one who has done good goes to a vile course, dear one.\n41. Reaching the worlds of those who have done merit, and dwelling there for everlasting years, one who has fallen from yoga is born in a house of the pure and the prosperous.\n42. Or he is born in a family of yogins, of the wise. For a birth of this kind is harder to gain in the world.\n43. There he gains that union with the understanding which belonged to the former body. And from that he strives still more toward perfection, O joy of the Kurus.\n44. By that former practice he is carried along even against his will. Even one who only wishes to know yoga passes beyond the Brahman of the word.\n45. The yogin who strives by effort, his sins washed clean and perfected through many births, then goes to the highest course.\n46. The yogin is higher than ascetics, and he is deemed higher also than the learned. The yogin is higher than ritualists as well. Therefore become a yogin, O Arjuna.\n47. Of all yogins, he who worships me with faith, his inner self gone to me, is deemed by me the most yoked.",
